<Layout title="Our journal & editorial principles — OYADO"><section class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">INDEPENDENT / CONSIDERED / CURIOUS</p><h1>A little closer<br/>to <em>Japan.</em></h1></section><article class="about prose"><h2>A journal in the making</h2><p>{demo?'OYADO is an English-language editorial prototype exploring Japanese places to stay. The current five guides are clearly labeled fictional design samples, not recommendations, reviews or booking listings.':'OYADO is an independent English-language journal of Japanese places to stay. Our first collection follows five properties selected from a saved regional draw. Articles use published sources and AI-assisted drafting; they are not reports of personal stays.'}</p><h2>Evidence before adjectives</h2><p>{demo?'Real properties are researched separately.':'We research official property information and keep uncertain details out of published guides.'} Each factual statement needs a source, retrieval date, confidence and verification status. A search result is a lead, not confirmation. Names and locations must match official information before a URL is accepted.</p><h2>People, not invented voices</h2><p>Owner interviews remain hidden until an interview is conducted, consent is recorded and the text is approved. We never invent quotations or imply we have stayed at a property.</p><h2>Images & access</h2><p>Our photography carries verified reuse licenses, linked photographer credits and capture years. Destination scenery is labeled separately from property photographs. Images may be resized and cropped for display; derivatives retain the stated image license. Original typography and CSS remain as a fallback. We respect access restrictions and never bypass authentication or CAPTCHAs.</p><h2>Human approval</h2><p>AI-assisted guides begin as drafts. Only verified live guides with a recorded human review may enter production. Practical details can change; travelers should check with the property before booking.</p></article></Layout>
